---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';import {evidence,url} from '../lib/data';
import analysis from '../../data/processed/analysis.json';
const dogs=analysis.researchUse.find(r=>r.species==='Dogs')!;
const findings=[
{title:'Federal protections changed. Their reach remains limited.',text:'The legal record includes anti-fighting offenses, the PACT conduct offense, big-cat restrictions and permission to use non-animal testing methods. Exceptions and agency implementation determine their actual reach.',confidence:'High confidence in legal changes',limit:'A stronger statute does not establish fewer harmed animals.',id:'cr-pact-2019'},
{title:'Recorded research use fell for dogs. Attribution is unresolved.',text:`Reported dogs used in covered research fell from ${dogs.values[0].value.toLocaleString('en-US')} in FY2021 to ${dogs.values.at(-1)!.value.toLocaleString('en-US')} in FY2025 (${Math.abs(dogs.percentChange!).toFixed(1)}%). This sums use categories C, D and E, excluding animals held but not used.`,confidence:'High confidence in the transcribed comparison',limit:'Not a constant facility cohort; major species are excluded. This is not an estimate of FDA reform’s effect.',id:'rw-exclusions'},
{title:'Horse inspections reveal a detection problem.',text:'In FY2022, industry-designated inspectors recorded 6.80 noncompliances per 100 inspected performance-horse entries with APHIS present, versus 1.79 without it. The direction is the same in all six reviewed years.',confidence:'High confidence in the recorded comparison',limit:'Groups were selected and may overlap; this is not a randomized oversight effect or a national cruelty rate.',id:'em-policy-hpa'},
{title:'Changing measurement can resemble changing welfare.',text:'General NIBRS population coverage rose from 33.9% in 2017 to 87.3% in 2024 for agencies reporting at least one month. Cruelty reports collected under that changing coverage cannot be read as a simple national trend.',confidence:'High confidence in coverage figures',limit:'These are general reporting figures, not animal-cruelty-specific completeness.',id:'ct-nibrs'},
{title:'There is insufficient evidence for an overall presidential ranking.',text:'The reviewed outcomes differ in severity, population, reporting and follow-up. Congress, agencies, courts, states and private actors share responsibility. No comparable estimate of suffering prevented was established in this review.',confidence:'High confidence in this review’s comparability limitation',limit:'Insufficient evidence to rank is not evidence that policies had no effect.',id:null}
];
---
<Layout title="What has federal policy accomplished?"><section class="hero"><div class="eyebrow">An evidence-based public guide</div><h1>What has federal policy<br/>actually done for animals?</h1><div class="hero-grid"><div><p class="lede">U.S. presidents and Congress have changed important protections. The evidence is much stronger on what became law than on how much suffering those policies prevented.</p><p>Explore selected federal actions from Clinton through both Trump administrations. See the provisions, implementation record, observed outcomes and limits on assigning credit.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href={url('policies/')}>Explore the policy record →</a><a class="button secondary" href={url('evidence/')}>Examine the data</a></div><p class="small">{evidence.policies.length} policy records · {evidence.sources.length} sources · Cutoff October 19, 2026<br/>Inventory counts describe this review, not policy impact.</p></div><aside class="inset"><h2>Four questions.<br/>Four different answers.</h2><ol><li><b>What changed in law?</b><br/>Read the scope and exceptions.</li><li><b>Was it implemented?</b><br/>Check enforcement and delays.</li><li><b>Did animal outcomes improve?</b><br/>Examine measured populations.</li><li><b>Did the policy cause the change?</b><br/>Test other explanations.</li></ol></aside></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="eyebrow">Executive summary</div><h2>What the evidence establishes</h2><div class="grid two">{findings.map((f,i)=><article class="card"><span class="number">0{i+1}</span><h3>{f.title}</h3><p>{f.text}</p><span class="tag">{f.confidence}</span><p class="small"><b>Limit:</b> {f.limit}</p><a href={f.id?url(`policies/${f.id}/`):url('comparison/')}>Inspect the evidence →</a></article>)}</div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="hero-grid"><div><h2>Give credit for the action.<br/>Keep effects open to scrutiny.</h2><p>Congressional development, a presidential signature, agency implementation and a court ruling are different contributions. A later outcome does not belong automatically to the president who signed the law—or the president in office when the outcome appeared.</p><a href={url('comparison/')}>Compare administration records →</a></div><aside class="inset"><h3>No “animals saved” counter</h3><p>The available records cannot support one. Registered animals are not rescued animals; removed animals need follow-up; declining legal trade does not establish declining killing.</p><a href={url('coverage/')}>See what is still unknown →</a></aside></div></section>
<section class="section" id="share-summary"><div class="eyebrow">A short summary to share</div><h2>Real protections. Uneven evidence of results.</h2><p class="lede">Federal animal-welfare laws and rules have expanded protections in specific areas, and enforcement has occurred. But records often count legal activity rather than animal suffering. Some measured changes are encouraging; missing coverage, changing enforcement and overlapping influences make presidential credit difficult to isolate. The defensible conclusion is to judge each policy by its coverage, implementation and animal outcomes—not by the number of laws signed.</p><div class="actions"><button id="copy-summary">Copy summary</button><a class="button secondary" href={url('sources/')}>Download the evidence</a></div><p id="copy-status" class="small" aria-live="polite"></p></section>
<script>document.querySelector('#copy-summary')?.addEventListener('click',async()=>{const t=document.querySelector('#share-summary .lede')?.textContent||'';try{await navigator.clipboard.writeText(t+'\n'+location.href.split('#')[0]);document.querySelector('#copy-status')!.textContent='Summary and page link copied.'}catch{document.querySelector('#copy-status')!.textContent='Copy is unavailable in this browser. Select the summary text to copy it.'}});</script></Layout>
